 The doctrine never rested on the verse. When the evidence showed it wasn't original, most of the church let it go.\n\nThe Old Testament has its own version of this story. The oldest complete manuscript of the Hebrew Bible, the Leningrad Codex, was copied in 1008. Then, beginning in 1947, Bedouin shepherds and later archaeologists brought hundreds of scrolls out of caves near Qumran, above the Dead Sea. Among them was a complete scroll of Isaiah, usually dated to the second century before Christ. It is about a thousand years older than any Hebrew copy of Isaiah known before. Most of its differences from the later text are spelling and small points of grammar.\n\nThe scrolls also showed that a few books, Jeremiah among them, circulated in more than one form before the text settled, and scholars are still working out what that means. That complication is real. But someone reading Isaiah 53 from that scroll is reading substantially the chapter you can read tonight.\n\nEhrman agrees with more of this than his readers sometimes realize. In a question-and-answer appendix to *Misquoting Jesus*, he said his position did not stand at odds with that of his old teacher, Bruce Metzger, that \"essential Christian beliefs are not affected by textual variants in the manuscript tradition of the New Testament.\" Metzger was an ordained Presbyterian minister. Ehrman became an agnostic. On that point, at least, they still agreed.\n\n## Who Chose the Books?\n\nThe second story is that the books were chosen late by men with power, and it is usually set at the Council of Nicaea in 325. Nicaea argued about whether the Son is fully God. It didn't draw up a list of books. The real history is slower and less dramatic. Around 180, Irenaeus, the bishop of Lyon, could argue that there are four Gospels, no more and no fewer, and treat it as settled. Paul's letters were being read as Scripture even earlier. Within the New Testament itself, 2 Peter sets them beside \"the other Scriptures,\" and admits along the way that \"there are some things in them that are hard to understand\" (2 Peter 3:16).\n\nThe first list we know of that names exactly the twenty-seven books of our New Testament comes from Athanasius of Alexandria, in his letter to the churches for Easter in 367. Getting there wasn't tidy. Some books, such as Hebrews, James and Revelation, were questioned for a long time in some places, and the church argued about them in the open. Its own historians wrote those arguments down, and they can still be read. The councils that came later mostly put in writing what the churches had already been reading for generations. The longer story is in [Who Decided What Books Are in the Bible?](/writing/who-decided-what-books-are-in-the-bible)\n\n## Did It Happen?\n\nCopying the words correctly doesn't make the words true. A lie can be preserved perfectly. So the next question is historical: do these books describe a real world, and do they describe it accurately? The Bible invites the question. Luke opens his Gospel the way a historian would:\n\n> \"Inasmuch as many have undertaken to compile a narrative of the things that have been accomplished among us, just as those who from the beginning were eyewitnesses and ministers of the word have delivered them to us, it seemed good to me also, having followed all things closely for some time past, to write an orderly account for you, most excellent Theophilus, that you may have certainty concerning the things you have been taught.\" (Luke 1:1–4)\n\nThat is a man who says he checked his sources. He also dates his story the way ancient historians did, by naming who ruled. John the Baptist began preaching in the fifteenth year of Tiberius, when Pontius Pilate governed Judea and Annas and Caiaphas held the high priesthood (Luke 3:1–3). Claims like that can be tested, and some have been.\n\nIn 1961, archaeologists at Caesarea Maritima, the seat of the Roman governors on the coast, found a limestone block, reused in a theater, inscribed with Pilate's name and his title, prefect of Judea. An inscription from Delphi in Greece places Gallio's term as proconsul of Achaia around AD 51 to 52. That gives historians one of the firmest dates in Paul's life, his hearing before Gallio in Corinth (Acts 18:12). In 1993, excavators at Tel Dan in northern Israel found part of a ninth-century BC Aramaic inscription in which a foreign king boasts of a victory over the \"House of David.\" As most scholars read it, it is the earliest mention of David's dynasty outside the Bible.\n\nArchaeology doesn't prove the Bible, and Christians who talk as if it does claim more than the evidence gives them. A stone with Pilate's name on it shows that Luke had the governor right. It says nothing about whether Jesus rose from the dead. Some questions are genuinely hard. The archaeology of the exodus and the conquest of Canaan is argued over intensely, and believing scholars disagree among themselves about how to read it. What the evidence does establish is narrower, and it still matters. These books are set among real rulers, offices and places, and where they can be checked, they have often held up.\n\nThe center of the New Testament is a claim archaeology can't reach. Writing to the Corinthians in the mid-50s, Paul passes on a summary he says he himself received. Christ died for our sins, was buried, was raised on the third day, and appeared to Peter and then to the Twelve (1 Corinthians 15:3–5). Then Paul adds a line that reads like an invitation to check: \"Then he appeared to more than five hundred brothers at one time, most of whom are still alive, though some have fallen asleep\" (1 Corinthians 15:6).\n\nA few verses later he stakes everything on it. \"And if Christ has not been raised, your faith is futile and you are still in your sins\" (1 Corinthians 15:17). The evidence for that claim needs its own essay, and you'll find it in [Did the Resurrection Actually Happen?](/writing/did-the-resurrection-happen) The New Testament doesn't ask to be excused from history. Its central claim is one that could be proved false.\n\n## What About the Contradictions?\n\nThen there are the apparent contradictions, which is where Ehrman's trouble started. Many of them are between the Gospels, and the differences are real. Read the four accounts of Easter morning side by side. Matthew has Mary Magdalene and \"the other Mary\" at the tomb, and an angel sitting on the stone. Mark has three women and a young man in a white robe inside the tomb. Luke has a larger group of women and two men in dazzling clothes. John tells it through Mary Magdalene, who later sees two angels where the body had been. A Christian who says these differences aren't there hasn't read the chapters closely.\n\nThe church has always known this. In the second half of the second century, an Assyrian Christian named Tatian wove the four Gospels into one continuous account, the *Diatessaron*, and for a long time it was the Gospel many Syriac-speaking churches read. In the fifth century Theodoret, the bishop of Cyrrhus, wrote that he had found more than two hundred copies of it in the churches of his region, and that he had collected them and put the Gospels of the four evangelists in their place. Around 400, Augustine wrote a long work on the agreement of the Gospels, answering critics who had already spotted the differences people point to now. The church had a single, smooth account of Jesus within reach, and it kept the four that don't line up perfectly.\n\nThat choice makes sense once you remember what testimony usually looks like. Several people describing the same confusing morning will each remember it a little differently, and investigators grow suspicious when witnesses agree too neatly. Ancient biographers also arranged material by theme, compressed events and summarized speeches as a matter of course, and the Gospel writers wrote within those habits.\n\nThat doesn't make every difficulty disappear. Mark 2:26 still has no explanation everyone accepts. Some scholars point out that Mark uses the same Greek construction later in his Gospel to mean \"in the passage about\" (Mark 12:26), so the phrase may name a section of Scripture rather than a date. Others find that unconvincing. But on what matters most, the four agree. Jesus was crucified and buried, women found his tomb empty, and his followers became convinced they had seen him alive.\n\nMany evangelical churches confess that Scripture is without error. A careful modern statement of that conviction, the Chicago Statement on Biblical Inerrancy of 1978, was drafted by scholars who knew these passages well. It says in so many words that inerrancy is not negated by \"the topical arrangement of material, variant selections of material in parallel accounts, or the use of free citations.\" As its own framers stated it, the doctrine never required the four Gospels to tell the story the same way. Other churches that also confess Scripture as the word of God word the conviction differently, saying it is fully trustworthy in everything it sets out to teach, and faithful Christians still divide over which statement is right.\n\nBut a great many young Christians absorb a faith in which the Bible stands or falls with their ability to win every argument about every verse. When they meet a difficulty they can't solve, they think they have to choose between telling the truth and keeping Christ. We taught them that, usually without meaning to. The Bible never did. It even has one apostle admitting that another is sometimes hard to understand.\n\n## What About the Hard Passages?\n\nFor many skeptics the deepest problem with the Bible isn't that its words were changed. It's that they weren't, and some of them are hard to read: laws that regulate slavery, commands in Joshua to destroy whole towns, psalms that ask God to break the teeth of enemies. Those questions deserve more than a paragraph. One of them gets a full essay in [Why Didn't the Bible Just Ban Slavery?](/writing/why-didnt-the-bible-ban-slavery)\n\nA reader who asks them has moved from \"Do we have the text?\" to \"Is the God of this text good?\" That is a better question. The Bible asks it too. Job asks it chapter after chapter, and Jeremiah and Habakkuk put it to God to his face.\n\nSome distrust of the Bible was earned, though not by the book. It was earned by people who quoted it to defend what they wanted, or used it as a club. Many of us have done a smaller version of the same thing. We reach for the verses that support us and pass over the ones that would correct us. If your questions about the Bible arrived with a loss or a wound and not only with an argument, you don't have to sort them out alone. If the wound came from abuse by a church leader, that abuse should be reported to the authorities. [Find Help](/help) is a place to start.\n\n## Why It Comes Back to Jesus\n\nThere is a way of defending the Bible that runs in a circle: it's true because it says it's true. The first Christians couldn't have reasoned that way, because they had no New Testament. They had the Scriptures of Israel and the news that Jesus had been raised. They trusted Jesus because they believed God had vindicated him by raising him from the dead, and then they received the Scriptures the way he did.\n\nJesus treated the Scriptures as the word of God. In an argument with his opponents he rested his case on a phrase from Psalm 82 and said that \"Scripture cannot be broken\" (John 10:35). But he also told critics who knew the Scriptures as well as anyone that they had missed what the book was for. \"You search the Scriptures because you think that in them you have eternal life; and it is they that bear witness about me, yet you refuse to come to me that you may have life\" (John 5:39–40).\n\nAfter his resurrection, walking with two of his followers on the road to Emmaus, \"beginning with Moses and all the Prophets, he interpreted to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning himself\" (Luke 24:27). The Bible's own account of itself is that it points to a person.\n\nThe manuscripts, the history of the canon and the archaeology can clear away bad reasons for dismissing the book, and they do. They can't make you trust it, any more than a well-documented biography makes you trust the man it describes. Christians trust the Bible because they have come to trust the one it is about. You could settle every question about Abiathar and still have to decide what to do with Jesus. Plenty of believers have carried unanswered questions about hard verses for a lifetime and trusted him the whole way.\n\n## What Trust Looks Like\n\nIf you're a skeptic, I'd ask one thing of you. Read one Gospel whole, in a sitting or two, the way you would read any ancient source you wanted to understand. Mark is the shortest. Notice what it keeps that anyone managing a reputation would have cut: disciples who don't understand, Peter's denial, women running from the empty tomb too afraid to speak. You may finish unconvinced. But you'll be arguing with the book and not with a rumor about it. The [Study Bible](/study/bible) is a good place to read it with notes beside you.\n\nFor those of us who already say we trust it, the harder question is whether our lives show it. A church can defend the reliability of the Bible loudly and read it very little. It can also quote it constantly while almost never letting it correct anything. Scripture itself expects its readers to get it wrong, which is why it keeps sending prophets to people who already have the Scriptures. That is the argument of [The Bible Assumes You Will Be Wrong](/writing/the-bible-assumes-you-will-be-wrong). Trusting the Bible means letting it tell us where we are wrong, and then changing.\n\nThe two on the road to Emmaus would have heard the Scriptures read all their lives. What they lacked was the one who could open them. Afterward they asked each other, \"Did not our hearts burn within us while he talked to us on the road, while he opened to us the Scriptures?\" (Luke 24:32).\n\nWhatever you make of the manuscripts, there is a question underneath them. It isn't only whether the book was copied well, though it was. It's whether the one it bears witness to is alive.\n\nIf he is, you can trust him with the hard verses too.\n\n*\"The grass withers, the flower fades, but the word of our God will stand forever.\"* (Isaiah 40:8)\n\n*\"These are written so that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that by believing you may have life in his name.\"* (John 20:31)\n",
   "updated": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"
 }
