 Paul's verb in Romans 13:1 is "be subject," the word for taking your place under an order. Peter's word in Acts 5:29 is the ordinary word for obeying a ruler. The two overlap, and Paul can ask for both at once, telling Titus to remind Christians "to be submissive to rulers and authorities, to be obedient" (Titus 3:1). But submission is the larger word. A Christian can refuse a command and still remain under the authority that gave it, accepting its penalty instead of fleeing or fighting. Martin Luther King Jr. pointed to the three men in Babylon in his letter from the Birmingham jail in 1963 and wrote that "one who breaks an unjust law must do so openly, lovingly, and with a willingness to accept the penalty."
 
